<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="recitation-phase-diagrams">
  <title>Autonomous Equations</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A first-order autonomous equation has the form
      <md>
        <mrow> \frac{dy}{dt} = f(y). </mrow>
      </md>
      In class, we introduced the phase diagram.
      In this recitation, you should think carefully about the relationship between the graph of <m>f(y)</m>, the phase diagram of the differential equation, and the classification of the equalibrium points as stable, unstable, or semistable.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <page>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider the equation <m>\frac{dy}{dt} = (y - 1)(y - 2)^2(y - 3)</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>


      <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch the graph of <m>f(y)</m>.
            Your sketch should have the correct zeros of the function, and should correctly indicate where <m>f(y)</m> is positive and negative.
            (Note: the horizontal axis should be <m>y</m> here.
            We don't have a label for the vertical axis.)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>


      <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Draw the <m>ty</m>-plane, which is where solution functions should be graphed.
            Draw the equilibrium solutions.
            Along the <m>y</m>-axis, put appropriate up- and down-arrows, and complete the phase diagram.
            Classify each equilibrium point as stable, unstable, or semistable.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>


      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Describe how the graph of <m>f(y)</m> could give the up- and down-arrow phase diagram information.
            What features in the graph of <m>f(y)</m> can you recognize as indications of stable, unstable, or semistable equilibrium solutions?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>


  <page>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          One model of population growth is:
          <md>
            <mrow> \frac{dP}{dt} = f(P) = rP\left(\frac{P}{T} - 1\right)\left(1 - \frac{P}{K}\right), </mrow>
          </md>
          where <m>r, K, T</m> are positive constants with <m>T \lt K</m>.
          This model is meant to capture the idea that there is some threshold <m>T</m> below which the population can't sustain itself, and there is a carrying capacity <m>K</m> above which the environment doesn't have sufficient resources for the population.
        </p>
      </introduction>


      <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch a graph of <m>f(P)</m>.
            Without drawing a phase diagram, identify the equilibrium solutions to the differential equation and classify them as stable, unstable, or semistable.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>


      <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Draw the phase diagram and compare to your graph of <m>f(P)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>